  "usage": "Jason X's team, precomputed from this snapshot so the report does not have to rebuild it. starters, bench and injured_reserve are the current lineup; open_starting_slots and capacity give the roster-spot answer directly, and has_open_bench_spot is the flag that decides whether an add needs a drop. bye_pressure lists which starters are on bye in each of weeks_ahead. injury_designations is every designated player on the roster with the expected return. Every player carries bye_week, their upcoming opponents and projected_points, which is ESPN's projection for the current week and the only forward-looking number on the roster; a 0.0 is usually a bye or a player not expected to play rather than a missing value. faab_plan costs the bid tiers against the remaining balance so an unaffordable bid cannot be recommended, and current_matchup gives this week's opponent with the risk posture it implies, plus my_lineup_risk and opponent_lineup_risk: each side's starters who are designated or on bye, and the projected points hanging on game-time decisions. Players whose game has started are left out of those. season_outlook is the standings against the playoff cut line: cushion_games is games ahead of the first team out (negative when behind the last team in), status and guidance say what that implies, and standings lists all teams. bye_calendar runs from this week to the end of the season, naming each starter on bye, the best bench cover chosen on rest-of-season projection, and uncovered_slots where the roster has no answer. ir_outlook says what happens to the roster when each fantasy-IR player is activated: a swap with a player on NFL IR, an open bench spot, or a forced drop; swap_at_risk means the swap partner is due back no later. ros_projected_points is ESPN's rest-of-season projection.",
